{"kind":"artifact","text":"The browser asks the publish-authority question at every point the path can still be stopped, including the one that signs. Exact clean pushed head 97a749ecd8a27e57eb6c301f822f105bda391249 on request/planner-dr-recut; tree ad191b80cb5cd5edd11bd632f9dc029cc8e0968d, checkout clean, local and origin identical. Successor to d4748f59 at this path.\n\nWHAT CHANGED RELATIVE TO THE WITHDRAWN 05c39532: NOTHING. That is the point of this head, and I proved it rather than asserting it. git diff 05c39532 HEAD is empty, and both commits carry tree ad191b80cb5cd5edd11bd632f9dc029cc8e0968d. What changed is provenance: the publish-authority recheck is behaviour discovered during review and outside the scope of b03f55fe, so it now rests on ede16d22, the child that authorizes it. The parent commit e1798050 is untouched and keeps resting on b03f55fe, which did authorize the lane carry — each commit rests on what actually authorized it rather than one trailer being stretched over both.\n\nTHE BEHAVIOUR, for a reader who has not followed the lane. TopBar gated the dialog opener with session.live \u0026\u0026 isLiveParticipant and nothing asked again afterwards. PublishArtifact received basis, busy, error, close and onPublish — no authority input — and its readiness was three non-empty strings. App.publish signed behind only `if (filing || awaiting) return`, dependency array [filing, awaiting, session.credential]. So a session that expired, or a membership superseded, while the dialog stood open still reached api.act. authority.ts now exports publishRefusal(live, actors, me), which asks the existing isLiveParticipant and returns the refusal reason or undefined; it is asked at the opener, inside the dialog, and again in App.publish in the same statement sequence as the signature. Toolbar.tsx is untouched at blob 506593d434f35251e79cefc261919d57bdeced40, and own-author withdraw stays ungated with its test.\n\nMUTATION WITNESS, REPRODUCED BY ME rather than carried over from claude's account. I removed only the four-line publishDenied guard from App.publish, leaving the dialog gate intact. Result: 100 tests, 99 pass, 1 fail. The failing assertion is in \"publish stops the moment authority does, at the dialog and at the boundary that signs\", message \"false offer: App signed a state record for an absent session, and the fold refuses that record as ineffective\", failing 1 !== 0 — it counts signed calls, not disabled attributes, which is why it catches a control that still submits. I restored the file and confirmed sha256 0a2901033813fc9973276a371534b3915f48f2ad64ad8db35350f1939f1e509a before and after, with git status empty.\n\nCONDITION (8), DERIVED AT THIS HEAD, AND THE ANSWER IS YES — THERE ARE TWO MORE. Claude enumerated the act:\"state\" sites and named App.tsx and Thread.tsx. That framing hides one. Asking instead where the browser actually signs, there are three api.act call sites in ui/src:\n\n  App.tsx:143  the artifact publish path      — GUARDED by publishRefusal as of this head.\n  App.tsx:76   doAct                          — NOT guarded. Its only gate is `if (inFlight.current.has(intent)) return`, dependency array [session.credential]. It is the signing site for Toolbar's ratify affordances, which submit act:\"ratify\" rather than act:\"state\" — which is exactly why an act:\"state\" enumeration missed it. Toolbar gates the offer with canRatify/mayRatify, so the authority question is asked when the row renders and never again at signing: the same time-of-check/time-of-use shape this head fixes for publish.\n  Thread.tsx:592  the composer                — NOT guarded at the signing boundary. send() returns early on `!text.trim() || busy || !session.actor || !ready`. session.actor is an identity, not liveness or membership. Thread does consult session.live, but only at line 706 on the submit button's disabled attribute — a screen fact, not a signing fact, and the same thing that proved insufficient for publish.\n\nI am naming these and not fixing them. Fixing them would be new behaviour outside ede16d22, which is the precise error that cost this lane its last three cycles. They want a child request, and I would rather the reviewer see the finding stated honestly than see me repeat the mistake the finding is about.\n\nGATES AT THIS EXACT HEAD, from runs I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok, zero failures. npm test --prefix ui 100 tests, 100 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty. go build and go vet exit 0. git diff --check exit 0. make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty, and make ui-check exit 0, so internal/service/uidist is byte-reproduced and the tree is still ad191b80 afterwards.\n\nONE GATE NOTE FOR WHOEVER RUNS THESE NEXT. My first npm run failed nine files with ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND for jsdom, react and vite. That was a fresh worktree with no ui/node_modules, not a code defect; npm ci --prefix ui fixed it and the suite then passed 100/100. I record it because a UI gate failing in a new worktree looks like a regression and is not one.","body":{"commit":"97a749ecd8a27e57eb6c301f822f105bda391249","path":"ui"}}